# /usr/lib/sysusers.d/weftdb.conf — the account and group a host install of WeftDB runs as.
#
# Part of PAC-11 (1.0 plan): the systemd unit, the user and the env file. Installed by
# `scripts/packaging/install-host.sh` next to the unit; `systemd-sysusers` is the command that
# reads it, and it is idempotent — running it twice, or on a machine where the operator already
# made the account by hand, changes nothing and says nothing.
#
# Why a sysusers.d file instead of `useradd` in the installer. Three reasons, and the third is
# the one that matters.
#
#   1. It is declarative: the account exists because a file says it should, not because a script
#      ran once, so an image build, a reinstalls after a package update, and a machine rebuilt
#      from a backup all agree about what the account is.
#   2. It is uid-stable across distros: `-` asks systemd to pick. Hard-coding a uid would collide
#      with something on somebody's machine, and a service whose uid changed between two boots
#      owns a data directory it can no longer open.
#   3. `systemd-sysusers` runs at package-install time *and* on every boot from
#      `systemd-sysusers.service`. A `useradd` line in a shell script runs at one of those two,
#      and the second time the machine disagrees with the account.
#
# The account is a service account in the sense that matters: no password, no login shell, and a
# primary group with no members other than the account itself. `nologin` rather than `false`
# because `systemd-sysusers` warns over `nologin` on machines whose `passwd` database is not local
# (LDAP, `systemd-homed`) and warns over nothing else; `nologin` refuses the login exactly as
# `false` does and leaves the account's shell in `/etc/shells`-independent shape.
#
# # 2. The lines
#
# Fields are `type name id geco home shell`. `u` is a user (and creates the group of the same
# name, and the same gid, if neither exists). The only valid types are `u`, `g`, `m`, `r`, `~`
# and `!` — a line of any other type is skipped with an error on stderr, which is why the
# directories this file used to create are in `weftdb.tmpfiles` instead.
#
# The home is `/var/lib/weftdb` because that is what the unit declares with
# `StateDirectory=weftdb`; `weftdb.tmpfiles` and `StateDirectory=` are what create it, with the
# mode and owner the unit needs. `ProtectSystem=strict` in the unit means that directory is the
# only place outside `/usr` and `/etc` the process can write to at all.

u weftdb - "WeftDB time-series store" /var/lib/weftdb /usr/bin/nologin
